# Lesson 05 of the Plasm Koans. Each row is one koan: what the learner discovers,
# and the acceptance scenario of Plasm it teaches (feature: scenario).
Feature: About stored records
  Domain types are ordinary immutable F# records and unions. A stored record names its table with [<Table>], and everything else follows from its types: its key is its field named Id, a column is named after its field, and ids, validated values and fieldless unions are stored by convention, with no mappings. A model is built once from the record types, before any query runs, and refuses what the types leave unclear.

  Prerequisites: lesson 04.

  Background:
    Given the koans database with its starting data

  Scenario Outline: <koan>
    Given the koan "<koan>" in this lesson
    Then it passes with its reference answer and fails until its blanks are filled
    And it teaches the acceptance scenario "<teaches>"

    Examples:
      | koan                                                | the learner discovers                                                                                         | teaches                                                                        | hint                                                                                                                                                                   |
      | A stored record is still plain F#                   | [<Table>] is a note on an immutable record: copy-and-update works as always                                   | elegance: Persistence metadata does not require mutable records                | The record named widget keeps the Name it was built with, and renamed has the Name the with expression gives.                                                          |
      | A record names its table                            | [<Table "products">] says which table holds the records, and repo.Explain shows it                            | elegance: Persistence metadata does not require mutable records                | Compare the table named in the SQL fragment with the Table attribute on Product in Shop.fs.                                                                            |
      | Columns are named in snake_case                     | a field called PlacedAt is stored in the column placed_at, by rule                                            | elegance: Persistence metadata does not require mutable records                | Apply the comment's rule to the field PlacedAt, and ask which of the fragments has the spelling the rule gives.                                                        |
      | A key finds one record                              | a stored record's key is its field named Id; filtering on it finds exactly one Product                        | elegance: Domain scalar wrappers preserve their meaning                        | Find the row of the products table under Given whose id is the number in the where.                                                                                    |
      | A union is stored as text                           | by convention a fieldless union is stored as its case name in snake_case: 'cancelled' reads back as Cancelled | scalar-conventions: A fieldless union is stored as its case name in snake case | The SQL sets that order's status text, and the comment says how each case of OrderStatus is spelled as text.                                                           |
      | Wrapped keys keep their meaning                     | by convention a single-case union such as CustomerId is stored as the number it wraps, and comes back wrapped | scalar-conventions: A single-case union is stored as the type it wraps         | Find the row of the customers table under Given with that email, and wrap its id in the type the comment names.                                                        |
      | Stored values can be rejected                       | a Quantity is read back through Quantity.create, so a stored zero is refused with the field named             | scalar-conventions: A type with a create function is read through it           | The comment says whether the constructor accepts zero, and the error's Property is the name of the OrderLine field whose stored value was refused.                     |
      | Absent values are options                           | a nullable column is an option field; None is not an empty string                                             | core: Nullable values remain explicit                                          | Read customer_id and cancellation_reason in the fourth row of the orders table under Given: a NULL is the absent case of an option, and a value comes wrapped in Some. |
